Audit and repair the make/ installers against real containers

The per-OS installers in make/ had not been run end to end in a long time and were broken
on every platform. They are almost pure side effects (~238 sudo calls, ~40 network
downloads, symlinks into $HOME), so they were exercised inside throwaway containers for
Ubuntu 24.04, Arch, Amazon Linux 2023 and Termux. The harness lives in make/test/ (see
make/test/README.md); the full write-up is make/test/FINDINGS.md.

Results, full `make install` -> `make init` -> `make test` per distro:

  Ubuntu 24.04   before: installer died at line 331 of 480; 43 pass / 11 fail
                 after:  runs to the end, 58 steps completed; 52 pass / 2 fail
  Arch           before: yay build broken so every AUR package silently skipped, while
                         the run reported success; 53 pass / 0 fail
                 after:  yay builds, 90 steps completed, 0 failed
  Amazon Linux   before: pip, ripgrep and diff-so-fancy could not install
                 after:  exits 0 and means it, 32 steps completed, 0 failed
  Termux         before: three /etc/os-release errors, then exit 0 having done nothing
                 after:  dispatcher refuses with exit 1 and a clear message

The two remaining Ubuntu/Amazon Linux failures are one test, `neovim (nvim) runs`, and are
a container artifact: both install Neovim as an AppImage, which needs FUSE, and the
containers deliberately run without --device /dev/fuse. A separate appimage_check step
unpacks and runs the binary; it passes on both.

The six optional installers (aws, gcp, azure, kubernetes, llm, tui) reach no `make install`
path and are asserted by no test, which is why they had rotted unnoticed. They now have
their own `--phase extras` and are 0 of 13 failed on all four platforms.

Highest-impact defects found:

- modules/common/autojump.sh sourced autojump.bash under `set -u`. Reading an unset
  $XDG_DATA_HOME there is a *fatal* shell error rather than a non-zero return, so
  `load_autojump || true` could not catch it and install-ubuntu.sh died at line 331 of
  480. Everything below had never been installed on a fresh machine: docker, gh,
  terraform, packer, tig, gcc-14, imagemagick, silversearcher and more. Nine of Ubuntu's
  eleven test failures traced back here.
- Every module re-derived SCRIPT_DIR into the same variable name its caller was using, so
  the second and later `source` lines resolved to modules/common/modules/common/... and
  only the first module in each list ever loaded. Renamed to MODULE_DIR.
- `readonly NVM_VERSION` collided with nvm's own function-local of that name, so
  `nvm install 20` silently resolved to whatever node was already on PATH and still
  reported success.
- `make install` exited 0 no matter what: no failure aggregation existed, so a run with
  ten broken steps was indistinguishable from a clean one and could never gate anything.
  safe_exec now records failures and install_summary turns the tally into an exit code.
- Data loss: remove_env deleted a file *and* its backup (a missing && turned the restore
  into one `rm` with four operands), and install-aws.sh's relative `rm -rf aws/` deleted
  the repo's own aws config directory when run from the root.
- uv's installer appends `. "$HOME/.local/bin/env"` to ~/.zshrc, which init.sh symlinks
  into this repo, so `make llm` left the checkout dirty. Suppressed with
  UV_NO_MODIFY_PATH=1.
- gh is not in the Ubuntu, Debian or Amazon Linux 2023 archives, and install-tui.sh
  hardcoded `pacman -S github-cli` in what claims to be the generic TUI installer.
- llm 0.32 is unrunnable as published: it does a bare `import httpx` without declaring it
  while openai 3.x now pulls the renamed httpx2 distribution. Fixed with `--with httpx`.
- install-kubernetes.sh chmod +x'd and installed a kubectl it never downloaded, and
  overwrote its own correct arch detection with a hardcoded amd64 before fetching eksctl.
- Two installers wrote a 404 body to disk as an executable: `curl -LO` without -f exits 0,
  so Neovim installed a 9-byte "Not Found" page as the editor and diff-so-fancy was one
  API rate limit away from the same.

The test oracles were wrong too, which is worse than no tests at all: test-install.sh had
no platform scope (Amazon Linux reported 21 failures for tools its installer never
attempts, Termux failed 57 of 61) and did not set up PATH or nvm before asserting, which
was 5 false failures per distro.

Deferred with reasoning recorded in FINDINGS.md and filed separately: CI, the
install-termux.sh rewrite, the missing tool sets, and install-init.sh's unhandled distros.
